HOW_TO_READ = ("<b>How to read a box.</b> Top: the class name (dashed border = interface; &laquo;record&raquo; = a small immutable "
 "value; &laquo;enum&raquo; = a fixed list). Middle: its fields, the state it holds. Bottom: its methods. Everything outside the "
 "grey dashed frame at the bottom is <code>Main.java</code>; the six boxes inside it are <code>Extensions.java</code>, drawn to "
 "show that the whole of page 05 hangs off three interfaces. <b>The arrows.</b> "
 "Hollow triangle = implements. Filled diamond = owns: the limiter owns one KeyState per key and is the only thing that "
 "deletes one. Plain arrow = references or returns. Dashed green = handed in through <code>configure()</code>. Dotted blue "
 "= notifies, after the unlock. <b>Where state lives:</b> everything mutable is in KeyState &mdash; the tokens, the instant "
 "they were measured at, the window counters, the log, and the lock that guards all of them. The RateLimiter holds only the "
 "map of those states plus the three things it was handed. Rule and Decision are immutable, so they are shared freely and "
 "never locked. The algorithms hold no state at all: they are handed a KeyState and a Rule and do arithmetic on them, which "
 "is why one instance serves every key in the process.")

# ============================================================ page 04: the code
CODE_INTRO = ("Read the green comments above each class and method first &mdash; they say what the thing is for and what it "
 "guarantees, and that is the whole shape of the system in two minutes; then read the bodies for the mechanics. Each copy "
 "button copies that whole file: <code>javac Main.java Extensions.java FailureTests.java &amp;&amp; java FailureTests</code> "
 "must print ALL PASS, and <code>java Main</code> runs the demo and the hundred-thread race.")

# ============================================================ page 05: follow-ups and practice
IMPLEMENT = ('<div class="card"><div class="ch"><h3>0 &middot; Implement the system</h3><button class="timer" data-min="60">start 60:00</button></div>'
 '<div class="cb"><div class="prompt">"Build me a rate limiter. It sits in front of our API: given a key &mdash; a tenant, an API key &mdash; it says whether this call may go through right now, and it has to be right when a hundred threads ask about the same key at the same instant. I want working code, not a diagram. Go."</div>'
 'Before typing, write your six clarifying questions. Then type in this order: the Clock interface with a manual one for tests; the Rule and Decision records; KeyState with its own lock; the RateLimitAlgorithm interface and TokenBucket behind it; RuleResolver with a flat implementation; RateLimiter with its map, the lock-per-key helper and the charge-then-admit order in <code>allow</code>; the filter that turns a Decision into 429; and a main that fires a hundred threads at one key on a frozen clock.</div></div>')

FU = [
("Ops doubles a tenant's limit during a sale, and a new tenant must ramp up instead of starting at full speed. No restart.", "functional", 5,
 "The limits already live behind <code>RuleResolver</code>, so neither of these opens the limiter. TierRules keeps each tier's rule in an AtomicReference, so <code>setRule</code> takes effect on the next decision and never in the middle of one &mdash; which is why <code>allow</code> reads the rule exactly once into a local. The subtlety is lowering a limit: a bucket may be holding more tokens than the new burst permits, so <code>allow</code> clamps it down before charging, otherwise you have granted permits the new policy says do not exist. The ramp is a wrapper round the same interface: WarmUpRules remembers when it first saw a key and scales the rule's numbers from a tenth up to the whole limit over 30 seconds, leaving the tier rules underneath untouched.",
 sect(src, "class TierRules", "interface LimiterObserver") + "\n" +
 "// the two lines in allow() that make a live change safe\n"
 "Rule rule = rules.ruleFor(key);                          // read ONCE: one decision sees one rule\n"
 "if (s.tokens > rule.burst()) s.tokens = rule.burst();    // burst lowered: hold no more than the new one\n\n" +
 X("warm-up", "the leaky bucket")),

("A hundred threads hit one key at its limit at the same instant. Prove that exactly the limit gets through.", "non-functional", 10,
 "The race lives between reading \"one token left\" and writing \"zero\". In this design the refill, the check and the charge happen inside one lock, and that lock is on the key's own state, so no other thread can run in the gap. The proof: 100 threads wait on one latch with the clock frozen so no permit can be minted mid-race, the latch opens, every thread calls allow for the same key, and the test counts the admissions. Exactly 50 on a limit of 50, and the bucket ends at zero rather than negative. The clock must be frozen for the assertion to be exact; with a live clock the answer is 50-or-a-few-more and the test is worthless.",
 T("        // 3. a hundred threads", "        // 4. a key nobody has seen")),

("One lock per key. Does that scale, or have you just moved the bottleneck? What about a single hot key?", "non-functional", 10,
 "It scales, and the answer is arithmetic rather than opinion &mdash; move 8 has the sums: the hottest of 5,000 keys holds its lock for 100 microseconds in every 1,000,000, and no other key touches that lock. The bottleneck did not move to the map either, because the states live in a ConcurrentHashMap, which has no single lock of its own. The interesting half of the question is the genuinely hot key, one tenant carrying all the traffic. Even there, 100 threads arriving at the same instant leave the last one waiting 20 microseconds in front of 2 milliseconds of work it was gating, so it never shows up in a latency graph. If it ever did, rung one of the ladder removes the lock rather than splitting it: hold the whole bucket as one immutable value and swap it with compare-and-set, so a thread that loses recomputes against the winner's value instead of sleeping. ExtDemo prints the real cost of that &mdash; 200 threads, exactly 50 admitted, and how many swaps were lost and redone.",
 sect(src, "    public Decision allow(String key, long cost)", "    void refund(String key") + "\n" + X("rung one of the ladder", "shadow mode")),

("The limiter said yes, and then the call never happened — the downstream service threw, or a later check refused it. What happens to the permit?", "functional", 5,
 "It is given back. The order inside allow is charge first, then admit, because admitting is the irreversible half; that leaves exactly one thing to undo, and <code>refund</code> is it. A refund refills from elapsed time first and then adds the permits back capped at the burst, so it can never mint budget that was not earned, and an unknown key is ignored rather than created. The place this really shows up is layered limits: a tenant budget and a global budget charged in order, where a refusal by the global one refunds the tenant &mdash; otherwise the tenant loses permits for a request nobody served. The demo checks that exact number: 3 admitted through a global budget of 3, and the tenant still holding 7 of its 10.",
 sect(src, "    void refund(String key", "    long remaining(String key)") + "\n" + X("layered limits", "back-pressure")),

("A dashboard asks \"how many permits are left\" a thousand times a second, and there are ten million keys. Show me both.", "non-functional", 5,
 "The read is O(1) and needs no new structure: the bucket already holds the tokens and the instant they were measured, so the answer is a refill and a floor, under the key's lock for the moment it takes. A read that refills is still a write, which is why it takes the lock; an unknown key answers with a full burst and is deliberately not created, so a monitoring sweep cannot fill the map. Memory is the real question: about 250 bytes a key, so 1.2 MB at 5,000 keys and 2.5 GB at 10 million. So a scheduled task calls sweepIdle, which drops any key nobody has asked about for at least a window. That is lossless, and saying why is the senior move: a key idle for a whole window has already refilled to its full burst, so a fresh state is the same state. Two traps come with it. Deleting a key while another thread is deciding on it would swallow a spent permit, so the state is marked removed under the lock and a caller that finds a removed state fetches again. And a caller who never repeats a key &mdash; ten million invented API keys between two sweeps &mdash; can fill the map faster than the sweeper drains it, so in production the sweep runs on a short period and the map gets a hard maximum, past which an unknown key is refused; a limiter that runs out of memory is worse than a limiter that is strict.",
 sect(src, "    long remaining(String key)", "    int sweepIdle(long idleMs)") + "\n" +
 sect(src, "    int sweepIdle(long idleMs)", "    int size()") + "\n" +
 sect(src, "    private KeyState lockState(String key", "    private void publish(String key")),

("Which rate-limiting algorithms do you know, and which one would you actually ship?", "design", 10,
 "Five names, three real ideas. The token bucket &mdash; what this code ships &mdash; holds permits that refill with elapsed time, so it is O(1) in time and in memory, and it lets a client burst up to a stored depth, which is exactly how API plans are sold: 600 a minute, burst 100. The leaky bucket is not a different answer: as a <i>meter</i> it is the same arithmetic upside down, counting the arrival time of the next allowed call forward instead of counting tokens up, and block 9 of FailureTests proves it by having GCRA and TokenBucket admit the identical string of calls on the same clock. The one reading of \"leaky bucket\" that does behave differently is the <i>queue</i> that drains at a fixed rate, and that delays rather than refuses &mdash; it is <code>WaitingLimiter</code> in Extensions.java, not an algorithm. The window family is about accuracy at the edge: the fixed window is one counter and is wrong by 2x at the boundary, the sliding counter fixes that for one more counter and a few percent of error, and the log is exact and costs one timestamp per permit. So pick by endpoint rather than by fashion: token bucket at the front door, sliding window log on login, where five a minute has to mean five.",
 "// the menu, and what each one costs. P permits per window W, burst B.\n"
 "//\n"
 "//  algorithm                state per key          time    burst?  exact?  where it belongs\n"
 "//  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n"
 "//  token bucket             2 numbers              O(1)    yes     yes     the default: public APIs\n"
 "//  leaky bucket (meter)     1 number  -- Gcra      O(1)    yes     yes     same admissions, no float drift\n"
 "//  leaky bucket (queue)     a deadline per caller  O(1)    no      yes     shaping, not limiting: WaitingLimiter\n"
 "//  fixed window             1 counter + a stamp    O(1)    yes     NO      never alone: 2x at the boundary\n"
 "//  sliding window counter   2 counters + a stamp   O(1)    yes     ~2%     what most production limiters run\n"
 "//  sliding window log       P timestamps           O(k)    yes     yes     login, OTP: 5 a minute means 5\n"
 "//\n"
 "// Ship the token bucket, and say why in one line: O(1) in time AND memory, it does not have the\n"
 "// boundary bug, and burst is a knob separate from rate -- which is the shape a plan is sold in.\n\n"
 + T("        // 9. the leaky bucket", "        System.out.println(failures")),

("Someone spends their whole minute at 11:59:59 and their whole next minute at 12:00:00. Show me the bug and the fix.", "design", 10,
 "A fixed window is a counter plus the window it belongs to, and windows sit on an absolute grid &mdash; they reset on the second, the minute, the hour &mdash; so a client that knows the boundary can spend twice the limit across it. The test proves it: 10 calls through in 100 milliseconds on a limit of 5 a second. The fix that stays O(1) is the sliding window counter: count this window exactly, and charge a share of the previous window weighted by how much of it is still inside the last 60 seconds. One millisecond after the boundary the previous window counts almost in full, so the same burst is refused. It is approximate by a few percent and it is what most production limiters actually run. The exact answer, a timestamp per permit in a deque, is in the same file and costs memory proportional to the limit; keep it for a login endpoint.",
 sect(src, "class SlidingWindowCounter", "class SlidingWindowLog") + "\n" + T("        // 2. the fixed window", "        // 3. a hundred threads")),

("We run twelve pods. Right now each one grants the full limit, so the tenant gets twelve times what they pay for. Fix it.", "twist", 10,
 "The interface survives; only an implementation is added. A Redis-backed limiter runs the identical refill-and-deduct as one Lua script, and Redis's single thread is the fleet's version of the per-key lock &mdash; the whole point is that the read and the write happen on the server, so there is no gap for twelve pods to race in. The reference code models that with a map behind one lock and the real script in the comment above it; the demo runs two pods against one budget of 50 and admits 50, not 100. Three things to say out loud: keep the local bucket in front as a cheap first filter so most refusals never leave the pod; the TTL on the key is the sweeper, so eviction comes free; and decide now whether a Redis outage fails open or fails closed, because that decision will be made at three in the morning otherwise.",
 X("twelve pods", "a limit per endpoint")),

("Different limits per endpoint, and a search costs more than a ping.", "twist", 5,
 "Both are smaller than they look. \"A limit per endpoint\" is a different key: tenant plus route, so the map grows by the number of routes and not one class changes. \"A search costs more\" is the cost argument that has been on <code>allow</code> since the first line, read from a small table of route to permits; a refusal for a cost of five reports a retry-after long enough to have earned five, not one. The thing worth flagging is cardinality: tenants times routes is the number the eviction sweep now has to keep up with, and it is the reason the sweeper exists rather than a nice-to-have.",
 X("a limit per endpoint", "warm-up")),

("Before we switch this on for a live tenant, ops wants a week of evidence that it will not break them. Give them that without refusing one request.", "twist", 5,
 "Shadow mode, and it is a wrapper rather than a change. <code>ShadowLimiter</code> takes any Limiter, runs the real algorithm and charges the real permits, then throws the refusal away and admits everything &mdash; while counting what it would have blocked. A week of that count on a dashboard is how you answer \"what will this break?\" with evidence instead of a guess. Switching it on for real is one wiring line, <code>new ShadowLimiter(limiter)</code> becoming <code>limiter</code>, and no class is opened to do it. The detail worth saying: it charges the permits for real, so the number it reports is the number you will get, not an estimate from a parallel bucket that has drifted away from the live one.",
 X("shadow mode", "layered limits")),

("A nightly batch job would rather wait for a permit than fail. Give it that, and tell me where you would refuse to offer it.", "twist", 5,
 "The decision already carries the answer: <code>retryAfterMs</code> is the shortest wait that can succeed, so waiting is a loop around the same call and needs no new state anywhere. <code>WaitingLimiter</code> takes a deadline the caller chose, asks, and on a refusal sleeps exactly the retry-after the limiter reported instead of polling on a fixed interval. At the deadline it returns false rather than waiting forever, which is the half people forget. Where I would refuse to offer it is a web request: a held thread under load is how a service falls over, because the queue in front of the limiter becomes the outage. So this is for batch jobs and queue consumers, and the web tier keeps failing fast with a 429.",
 X("back-pressure", "twelve pods")),

("Where does time come from, and how do you test a refill without a Thread.sleep?", "design", 5,
 "Time is handed in as a one-method interface. In production it is System.nanoTime rather than the wall clock, because only elapsed time matters here and nanoTime cannot jump backwards when NTP corrects the machine. In tests it is a clock the test sets, so \"200 milliseconds later\" is one line that runs instantly; the whole suite has no sleep in it. That is also what makes the nasty cases testable: the clock going backwards must mint nothing, and it must not mint anything for the interval it travelled twice either. The belt-and-braces clamp is still in the refill &mdash; elapsed of zero or less mints nothing &mdash; because a monotonic clock is a promise about production, not about whatever a caller injects.",
 sect(src, "interface Clock", "enum Tier") + "\n" + T("        // 1. burst then refill", "        // 2. the fixed window")),

("Which pattern is where, and why did each one earn its place?", "design", 5,
 "Nothing here was chosen up front, which is why every name can be defended in one sentence. Strategy is move 3: the algorithm is a one-call interface handed in through <code>configure</code>, so the sliding window arrived as a new class and one changed line. Decorator appears twice and both times it wraps instead of copying &mdash; ShadowLimiter wraps a whole limiter, WarmUpRules wraps a rule source. Observer is move 4's rule that nothing slow may sit inside the lock, so decisions are announced after the unlock. State is move 6 and it is the one that earns its keep: writing FRESH, SPENDING, THROTTLED and IDLE down is what forced charge-then-admit. The two worth naming as <i>not</i> earned are Factory, which earns a place the day algorithms arrive as strings from a config file, and Singleton, which earns nothing at all here &mdash; one limiter per process is a wiring fact, and a static getInstance would make every test share a bucket.",
 "// Strategy: the rule that decides, behind an interface, handed in\n"
 "interface RateLimitAlgorithm { Decision tryAcquire(KeyState s, Rule rule, long cost, long nowMs); }\n"
 "void configure(RateLimitAlgorithm algo, RuleResolver rules, Clock clock) { ... }\n\n"
 "// Decorator: wrap the thing, do not copy it\n"
 "class ShadowLimiter implements Limiter { private final Limiter delegate; /* run it, then allow anyway */ }\n"
 "class WarmUpRules implements RuleResolver { private final RuleResolver base; /* scale its numbers by age */ }\n\n"
 "// Observer: announced after the unlock, each listener in its own try/catch\n"
 "private void publish(String key, Decision d) {\n"
 "    for (LimiterObserver o : observers) { try { o.onDecision(key, d); } catch (RuntimeException ignored) { } }\n"
 "}\n\n"
 "// State: a key's life, and the order it forces inside allow()\n"
 "// FRESH -> SPENDING -> THROTTLED -> SPENDING -> IDLE (swept).  Charge, then admit. Refund undoes the charge.\n\n"
 "// Factory: not yet. It earns its place the day this line is needed:\n"
 "Map<String, Supplier<RateLimitAlgorithm>> registry = Map.of(\"token_bucket\", TokenBucket::new, \"sliding_log\", SlidingWindowLog::new);\n"
 "RateLimitAlgorithm algo = registry.get(config.get(\"algorithm\")).get();\n"),

("Which SOLID letter is where in this code?", "design", 5,
 "The honest version of this answer is short, because SOLID here is a check on the moves and not a goal anyone designed for. S: TokenBucket changes when token arithmetic changes, RateLimiter when the key lifecycle changes, TierRules when the plans change, and none of them does two of those. O: shadow mode and the second global budget each arrived as a new class plus one wiring line, and TokenBucket &mdash; the class most likely to hide a concurrency bug &mdash; has never been reopened. L: every Limiter promises the same three things (never admit more than the rule allows, never block, always return a usable retry-after), which is exactly why LayeredLimiter can hold a mixture of them with no <code>instanceof</code> anywhere. I and D are the same fact from two sides: Clock, RuleResolver and LimiterObserver have one method each, and they are handed in, which is what lets a test give the limiter a clock that jumps and a listener that always throws.",
 "// S: one reason to change each\n"
 "class TokenBucket { /* token arithmetic */ }   class RateLimiter { /* keys, locks, order */ }   class TierRules { /* policy */ }\n\n"
 "// O: new behaviour is a new class plus one wiring line; nothing was opened\n"
 "Limiter live = new ShadowLimiter(limiter);                 // day 1: measure\n"
 "Limiter live = limiter;                                    // day 8: enforce\n\n"
 "// L: a mixture of implementations, and not one instanceof\n"
 "new LayeredLimiter(List.of(new Layer(\"tenant\", perTenant, k -> k), new Layer(\"global\", global, k -> \"ALL\")));\n\n"
 "// I: one method each, so a fake is a lambda\n"
 "interface Clock { long nowMs(); }   interface RuleResolver { Rule ruleFor(String key); }\n\n"
 "// D: handed in, which is what makes the nasty tests possible\n"
 "limiter.configure(new TokenBucket(), new FlatRules(Rule.perSecond(5)), manualClock);\n"
 "limiter.addObserver((k, d) -> { throw new RuntimeException(\"the metrics sidecar is down\"); });\n"),

("An enum for the algorithm with a switch, or an interface? And where would a Factory actually pay here?", "design", 3,
 "Split it by whether the thing is data or behaviour. A tier is data &mdash; FREE, PRO and ENTERPRISE differ only in three numbers &mdash; so it is an enum with a rule per constant, and adding a plan is one constant and one row. An algorithm is behaviour, with its own fields and its own arithmetic, so an enum with a switch inside <code>tryAcquire</code> would put four unrelated implementations in one method and reopen it for every new one; that is an interface, and the sliding window proved it by arriving as a class rather than a case. A Factory pays the moment creation stops being a constructor call: algorithms named in a YAML file, a registry from string to constructor, so a new one is a registration rather than a growing switch. Until then it is one <code>new</code> in the wiring code and a factory would only hide it.",
 "// data: a tier is three numbers, so it is an enum plus a table\n"
 "enum Tier { FREE, PRO, ENTERPRISE }\n"
 "rules.setRule(Tier.PRO, Rule.perMinute(600, 100));\n\n"
 "// behaviour: an algorithm has its own state and its own arithmetic, so it is a class behind an interface\n"
 "class SlidingWindowCounter implements RateLimitAlgorithm { /* arrived without reopening anything */ }\n\n"
 "// NOT this: one method that must be reopened for every new algorithm\n"
 "enum Algo { TOKEN_BUCKET, FIXED_WINDOW;\n"
 "    Decision tryAcquire(...) { switch (this) { case TOKEN_BUCKET: ...; case FIXED_WINDOW: ...; } } }\n\n"
 "// the Factory earns its place when the name comes from configuration\n"
 "Map<String, Supplier<RateLimitAlgorithm>> registry = Map.of(\"token_bucket\", TokenBucket::new, \"gcra\", Gcra::new);\n"),
]

